# Part 3. Module 1 in detail

## Lesson 1: What is Version Control?
### 1\. First, understand the problem

Imagine that you have five developers working on one website.

- Developer A is working on the login page.
- Developer B is working on the registration page.
- Developer C is working on the payment page.
- Developer D is working on the home page.
- Developer E is working on the database connection.

Now imagine all five developers are editing the same project without any version control.

What can happen?

1. One developer may overwrite another developer's code.
2. Nobody knows who changed a particular line.
3. It becomes difficult to recover deleted code.
4. Developers may have different versions of the project.
5. Combining everyone's work becomes difficult.

Now the question is?

"If your friend accidentally deletes 100 lines of your project, how will you recover them?"

This question introduces the need for Version Control.

### 2\. What is Version Control?

Version Control is a system that records changes made to files over time.

It helps developers\:

- Track who made changes.
- Understand what was changed.
- Identify when changes were made.
- Restore earlier versions.
- Collaborate with other developers.

Think of it like the history feature in a document editor, but designed for software development projects.

### 3\. Types of Version Control Systems

There are three major types.

A. Local Version Control System

In this system, version history is maintained on one computer.
Example\:
Project
   |
   |--- Version 1
   |--- Version 2
   |--- Version 3
   |--- Version 4

Advantages\:

- Simple to understand.
- No network required.

Disadvantages\:

- Difficult to collaborate.
- If the computer fails, history may be lost.
- Not suitable for large development teams.

B. Centralized Version Control System (CVCS)
In this system, a central server maintains the repository.

Example: SVN (Subversion).

              Central Server
              (Repository)
                   |
          ---------------------
          |        |          |
       Dev A     Dev B      Dev C

All developers connect to a central server to access and update project files.

Advantages\:

- Centralized management.
- Easier collaboration.
- Central repository contains project history.

Disadvantages\:

- Central server failure affects the team.
- Network connectivity is generally required to access central repository operations.

C. Distributed Version Control System (DVCS)
This is where Git comes in.
Every developer has a complete local copy of the repository, including its history.

Example\:
              GitHub Remote
                   |
          ---------------------
          |        |          |
       Dev A     Dev B      Dev C
       Local     Local      Local
       Repo      Repo       Repo

Each developer can:

- Work offline.
- Create commits locally.
- Create branches.
- View project history.
- Later synchronize changes with the remote repository.

Important point: Git is distributed. GitHub is a hosting and collaboration platform for Git repositories. Git can work without GitHub.

## 4\. What is Git?

Git is A free, open-source distributed version control system used to track changes in source code and other files.

Official website: [git-scm.com](https://git-scm.com/)

Git was created by Linus Torvalds in 2005, initially to support Linux kernel development.

Why was Git created?

The Linux kernel development team needed a fast, distributed, and reliable version control system.

Git was designed to provide:

- Speed.
- Distributed development.
- Data integrity.
- Branching and merging.
- Reliable history management.

## 5\. What is GitHub?

GitHub - A cloud-based platform for hosting Git repositories and collaborating on software projects.

Official website: [github.com](https://github.com/)

GitHub provides features such as\:

- Repository hosting.
- Pull Requests.
- Code reviews.
- Issues.
- Project management.
- GitHub Actions.
- Security scanning.
- Team collaboration.

### Git vs GitHub – make sure students understand this

| Git | GitHub |
| Version control system | Online hosting platform |
| Installed on computer | Accessed through web or Git clients |
| Works offline | Online collaboration platform |
| Tracks file changes | Hosts Git repositories |
| Command-line tool | Web interface and developer services |
| Developed by Linus Torvalds | Launched in 2008 |

##2A simple analogy:

Git is like a camera that records every change in your project.

GitHub is like an online platform where you can store and share that project with other people.

# Part 4. First classroom practical

(Practical 01) - Install and configure Git

We will use Windows PowerShell and Ubuntu/Linux terminals for demonstrations. These will also fit our existing DevSecOps teaching environment.

### Step 1. Check whether Git is installed

On Windows PowerShell:
On Ubuntu:
git --version

If Git is installed, you will see a version number.

### Step 2. Install Git

Windows\:

Download from: - [Git for Windows – Official Download](https://git-scm.com/downloads/win)

Ubuntu - 

sudo apt update
sudo apt install git -y

Verify\:
git --version

### Step 3. Configure Git identity

Explain to students:

Whenever we create a Git commit, Git records the author's name and email address.

We configure these details before creating commits.

git config --global user.name "Kunwar Rajesh"
git config --global user.email "kunwarrajesh2407@gmail.com"

### Step 4. Verify configuration
git config --list

Expected output will include\:
user.name=Kunwar Rajesh
user.email=kunwarrajesh2407@gmail.com

### Step 5. Understand configuration levels

Git has three important configuration levels:

| Level | Meaning |
| System | Applies to all users of the system |
| Global | Applies to the current operating-system user |
| Local | Applies only to the current repository |

Commands\:
git config --system
git config --global
git config --local

For actually viewing the settings:

git config --global --list
git config --local --list

Local settings override global settings, and global settings override system settings.

### Step 6. Create your first project

mkdir git-training
cd git-training

Create an HTML file.

On Ubuntu:
nano index.html

On PowerShell:
notepad index.html

Add this content:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My First Git Project</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to Git Training</h1>
    <p>Learning Version Control System.</p>
</body>
</html>


Save the file.

At this point, we have created a project, but Git is not tracking it yet.

That leads us to our next practical: initializing a Git repository.
### Step 7. Initialize Git

Run\:
git init

Expected output:
Initialized empty Git repository

Explain\:

The `git init` command initializes a new Git repository inside the current project directory.

Git creates a hidden directory named `.git'.

Check it:
ls -la

On PowerShell:

Get-ChildItem -Force

You should see\:
git-training/
    .git/
    index.html

**Important\:** The `.git` directory is the heart of your Git repository. It stores configuration, references, objects and other information needed to maintain the project's history.

### Step 8. Check repository status

git status
We will see that `index.html` is an untracked file.

Explain the meaning\:
An untracked file is a file that exists in the working directory but has not yet been added to Git's tracking system.

### Step 9. Add the file to the staging area
git add index.html

Check again:
git status

Now the file is staged and ready to be committed.

### Step 10. Create your first commit

git commit -m "Initial commit"

Explain\:

A commit is a recorded snapshot of the project's tracked content at a particular point in time.

### Step 11. Check commit history
git log

For a compact view\:
git log --oneline

Congratulations\! Students have created their first Git repository and recorded their first commit.

# Part 5. The most important concept – Git's three areas
Git works with three important areas\:

1\. Working Directory
This is where developers create, edit and delete files.
Example\:

We open `index.html` and change the heading.

2\. Staging Area
This is where you select the changes that should be included in the next commit.

Command\:
git add index.html

3\. Local Repository

This is where Git stores the committed project history.

Command\:
git commit -m "Updated homepage"

The complete workflow\:

Working Directory*

Create or modify files

(git add)
Staging Area

Select changes for the next commit

(git commit)
Local Repository*

Save a permanent commit in project history
"Git does not automatically commit every change. We first select the changes using `git add`, and then save them using `git commit. This distinction is fundamental to understanding Git.

